00:00:03.590 --> 00:00:07.269 align:start position:0%
the numbers are staggering since january  covet 19 has killed over 197  000 brazilians more people have died in  four months  than in all of last year but now the  pandemic  has taken a sharp political turn  congress is investigating whether  president jair bolsonaro  is responsible for the world's second  largest covet 19 death toll  after the united states bolsonaro is  accused of downplaying the dangers of  the virus  and the efficiency of the vaccines  last year the government rejected  several offers to buy the coronavec  and pfizer vaccines from china and  united states  delaying the mass inoculation campaign  this is the first time bolsonaro's  criminal actions are being investigated  the government not only denied the  pandemic but also confronted governors  and mayors who imposed restrictions to  protect the population  the supreme court barred the president  from overturning curfews and lockdowns  but now congress will hold him  accountable for his actions  bolsonaro's allies failed to stop  lawmakers from launching the probe  now a senate committee will be looking  into at least  20 accusations among them the  government's failure  to have a national plan senators say  that the priority is to investigate  why brazil took so long to procure  vaccines  despite being latin america's largest  economy and having the technological  means  to produce them domestically but  government officials  will also be asked about the delay in  resuming emergency aid  to the country's growing poor  19 million people have gone hungry  during the past year  almost twice as much as in 2018  and half of the population is facing  food insecurity  not knowing when they'll have their next  meal  many like this couple have moved to the  street  after the government suspended the  emergency aid in january  handouts started being distributed again  this month  but they still have not received them  nor have their new neighbors  this is mr goshu he had a shop and lost  everything during the pandemic  like him there are dozens sharing a  sidewalk  the pandemic and the economic crisis it  brought about are far from over  with less than seven percent of  brazilians fully vaccinated  it will be a long time before life goes  back to normal  meanwhile lawmakers are hoping the  investigation  will determine what went wrong and who  was to blame  monika yanakiv al-jazeera rio de janeiro
 

